 	/**
 	 * Whether the stream is at or AHEAD of the state, which is the only position
 	 * from which an append cannot leave a hole. See `streamLastToBlock`.
+	 *
+	 * ## An UNKNOWN POSITION REFUSES -- and "no stream" is not an unknown position
+	 *
+	 * This used to open with `if (this.streamLastToBlock === undefined || !this.lastSync)
+	 * return true`, one permissive branch over two different facts. ADR-0087 asked for
+	 * both to refuse, on the grounds that a fold which never learned where the stream
+	 * reaches takes the permissive branch and `streamRemainderOf` then has nothing to
+	 * strip against, so a fold that is BEHIND the stream stores its whole overlap a
+	 * second time -- the hand-over it measured at 4 stored rows where 2 were correct.
+	 * The two halves were separated and each was MEASURED against the tree. They do not
+	 * get the same answer.
+	 *
+	 * **No cursor REFUSES.** A fold that cannot say where it is cannot say this batch
+	 * continues anything, so permitting was only ever an accident of the `||`. It is
+	 * unreachable as things stand -- `promiseToIndex` loads before it fetches and
+	 * `load` sets `lastSync` on every branch it returns through -- which is exactly why
+	 * it costs nothing to make the safe answer the written one before something reaches
+	 * it.
+	 *
+	 * **An absent `streamLastToBlock` is NOT an unknown position: it is the DOCUMENTED
+	 * absence of a stream**, and refusing there is wrong. `forgetStoredStream` sets it
+	 * and says so in those words -- "there is no stream on disk any more, so nothing
+	 * constrains the next write" -- and `load` reaches it on every branch where the
+	 * keeper answered with no stream, including the one that CLEARED a mismatched or
+	 * unreadable one. Two things follow, and the second is the measurement:
+	 *
+	 * 1. A fresh deployment's FIRST save is that case, so a refusal there means no
+	 *    stream is ever written at all.
+	 * 2. A stream lost while its STATE survived -- a self-clear, a browser eviction --
+	 *    is re-opened by the next save at the state's resume point, and the subtree
+	 *    RECORDS that resume point as its own `startBlock`. So it is not a HOLE: a read
+	 *    from below it is answered `does-not-reach-back` rather than served, which is
+	 *    what makes the partial stream an honest artifact instead of a cursor claiming
+	 *    coverage it never received. `packages/core/test/streamSeedInstall.test.ts`
+	 *    asserts that stream must survive, by name (`locallyIndexedAbove`), and
+	 *    refusing here left it never written and that suite red in two cases.
+	 *
+	 * So this half STAYS permissive, deliberately. The duplicate the ADR measured was
+	 * not on this path at all: the hand-over it describes writes through
+	 * `StreamBuilder.storeStream` over an `EmissionAppender` (ADR-0052), which is
+	 * APPEND-ONLY, reads nothing back and has no hole guard of any kind -- and this
+	 * method is `IndexerGeneration`'s, over an `ExistingStream` that `load` always
+	 * reads first. Whoever moves the write duty guards the RECEIVING side; there is
+	 * nothing to tighten here.
 	 */
 	protected streamCanReceive(): boolean {
-		if (this.streamLastToBlock === undefined || !this.lastSync) {
+		if (!this.lastSync) {
+			return false;
+		}
+		if (this.streamLastToBlock === undefined) {
 			return true;
 		}
 		return this.streamLastToBlock >= this.lastSync.lastToBlock;
